    "result_text": "Done. The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe change lives in `TelephoneE164.toE164(...)`, the single normalization point used by the create-owner pipeline (`RequireOwnerFields` \u2192 `normalize`, which throws `InvalidTelephoneException` \u2192 400).\n\nAfter the general 8\u201315 total-digit check, I added a per-country national-length check:\n\n```java\nprivate static boolean hasValidNationalLength(String e164) {\n    if (e164.startsWith(\"61\")) {\n        return e164.length() - 2 == 9;   // +61 \u2192 9 national digits\n    }\n    if (e164.startsWith(\"1\")) {\n        return e164.length() - 1 == 10;  // +1 \u2192 10 national digits\n    }\n    return true;                          // other codes: only the 8\u201315 rule\n}\n```\n\nNotes:\n- `+61` numbers must have exactly 9 national digits; `+1` numbers exactly 10. A wrong length returns `null`, so `normalize` throws `InvalidTelephoneException` and the handler responds **400**.\n- Other country codes (e.g. the `+64` example in the docstring) are unaffected \u2014 still bound only by the general 8\u201315 total-digit rule. `61\u2026` and `1\u2026` prefixes don't collide since `+64` starts with `6` (not `61`) and doesn't start with `1`.\n- The valid path is preserved: `uniqueTelephone()` (`04\u2026`, 10 digits) normalizes to `+61` + 9 national digits, which the new rule accepts.\n- I also updated the class Javadoc to document the per-country requirement.\n\nThe new acceptance test `errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` (`+61 123`) now fails validation on the national-length rule and returns 400.",
